WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.520
Hello everyone, welcome back to another episode of the Grand Prixquel podcast where we discuss

00:04.520 --> 00:06.080
all things feeder series.

00:06.080 --> 00:11.120
On today's episode we are going to be doing a recap of the F2 and F3 weekends at Monaco

00:11.120 --> 00:14.020
and the Eurocup 3 weekend at Portimao.

00:14.020 --> 00:21.760
Your hosts today are myself, Robin, Evan and Peter.

00:21.760 --> 00:26.840
I think it's best we look over to F2 at Monaco to begin with.

00:26.840 --> 00:31.040
In practice we had quite a few incidents in practice with both Goethe and Villagomez

00:31.040 --> 00:36.960
hitting the wall, but I mean in Monaco this sort of thing always happens, it's almost

00:36.960 --> 00:37.960
to be expected.

00:37.960 --> 00:44.400
This whole weekend was just Monaco-esque, I mean even in qualifying we had John Bennett

00:44.400 --> 00:47.200
hitting the wall as well.

00:47.200 --> 00:56.160
The quali session was so weird, because Camara had like damage or something and then because

00:56.160 --> 01:03.400
Bennett crashed, they pulled the red flag out and then Invicta had the time to fix Camara's

01:03.400 --> 01:04.400
car.

01:04.400 --> 01:05.400
How lucky.

01:05.400 --> 01:08.000
He's so lucky, it's actually insane.

01:08.000 --> 01:12.320
Didn't he hit the wall during his last flying lap?

01:12.320 --> 01:13.320
Or was that Slater?

01:13.320 --> 01:18.320
No, Freddie did, he hit it like three times, but he still came P2.

01:18.320 --> 01:21.840
I just remember it was one of them, I don't remember which one.

01:21.840 --> 01:28.520
Yeah, but Camara, I don't understand because it's not like he's been fast, I feel like

01:28.520 --> 01:35.520
Tsolov, he's just been on it every single race, whereas Camara, he's been there but

01:35.520 --> 01:37.840
not fully there, you know what I mean?

01:37.840 --> 01:43.560
And Dunne as usual was fast, finished second in the group.

01:43.560 --> 01:47.240
I mean Dunne, that's the thing with him, as long as he keeps it clean he can get really

01:47.240 --> 01:49.040
good results.

01:49.040 --> 01:52.000
He kept it clean this weekend, which is nice.

01:52.000 --> 01:53.000
For once.

01:53.000 --> 01:54.000
For once.

01:54.000 --> 01:57.320
First weekend since like what?

01:57.320 --> 02:00.400
In like six months he's kept it clean.

02:00.400 --> 02:03.280
Was it like Qatar last time he didn't DNF?

02:03.280 --> 02:08.840
Nah, I'm fairly sure he punted into Martins in Qatar or something happened.

02:08.840 --> 02:09.840
That was Abu Dhabi.

02:09.840 --> 02:14.880
Oh Abu Dhabi, yeah, but I mean the fact we have to go to last season for something is

02:14.880 --> 02:15.880
insane.

02:16.760 --> 02:22.400
We've had three rounds already this year and every single round something has happened.

02:22.400 --> 02:29.480
The Group B, I mean, given who was in Group B, I think it's fairly inevitable Tsolov

02:29.480 --> 02:30.480
will top it.

02:30.480 --> 02:35.280
We need to have serious conversations about Kush Maini.

02:35.280 --> 02:36.960
What is he doing?

02:36.960 --> 02:41.480
How is he with Alpine in the first place?

02:41.480 --> 02:43.800
The beautiful thing called money.

02:43.800 --> 02:49.240
How much does he have, because honestly this is like, I mean he's like Dunne but if he

02:49.240 --> 02:50.240
Was slow.

02:50.240 --> 02:53.440
Dunne but no redeeming qualities.

02:53.440 --> 02:55.880
Dunne but not actually Dunne, he's just bad.

02:55.880 --> 02:56.880
Dunne off Temu.

02:56.880 --> 03:02.120
The thing is, isn't this like Maini's like fourth year in F2 or something, third

03:02.120 --> 03:03.120
or fourth year?

03:03.120 --> 03:08.480
Yeah at least Verschoor, he had some form of pace, I'll give him that.

03:08.480 --> 03:13.640
Maini somehow like he doesn't seem to improve each year, if anything he gets worse somehow

03:13.640 --> 03:15.600
I don't know how that is possible.

03:15.600 --> 03:21.640
Like how do you crash into someone during qualifying? Poor Van Hoepen.

03:21.640 --> 03:23.840
The only time that line has ever been said.

03:23.840 --> 03:28.240
Yeah I mean there's not too much to say about qualifying I reckon, so I think we're going

03:28.240 --> 03:34.640
to the sprint which you know in Classic Monaco style is extremely uneventful.

03:34.640 --> 03:38.720
I think I nearly fell asleep during the sprint, or at least one of the races, because actually

03:38.720 --> 03:42.680
both of the races were pretty boring, but we'll get to the sprint first.

03:42.680 --> 03:48.560
I nearly fell asleep during basically everything, like the only thing I didn't fall asleep through

03:48.560 --> 03:49.560
was F1.

03:49.560 --> 03:54.240
Yeah I still haven't, I need to watch the F1 race actually but-

03:54.240 --> 03:59.240
Oh my god it was something, but we're not an F1 podcast, we're not talking about

03:59.240 --> 04:00.240
that.

04:00.240 --> 04:07.160
The thing is, historically Monaco for F2 and F3 has been semi-decent because the cars aren't-

04:07.160 --> 04:11.520
They’re a lot smaller than F1 cars, so you have been able to overtake, but it seems like this

04:11.520 --> 04:15.040
year they just weren't able to do anything.

04:15.040 --> 04:19.080
Maybe F2, I think maybe they’re still traumatized from last year.

04:19.080 --> 04:20.080
Maybe.

04:20.080 --> 04:27.080
Dunne went to the runoff I think during the sprint to avoid another Monaco ‘25 rerun.

04:27.080 --> 04:33.960
At least he's learning, so happy he's finally learning, but maybe if Victor Martins was there

04:33.960 --> 04:39.520
he'd have, he's not quite found his target for this year yet, maybe that's all it is.

04:39.520 --> 04:45.240
I heard apparently drivers are just being a lot more cautious now at Turn 1, although

04:45.240 --> 04:46.240
that's what was happening.

04:46.240 --> 04:50.000
So that's probably why we got nothing in the carnage this year, which is really annoying.

04:50.000 --> 04:54.960
We didn't get any heroics into Sainte Devote, which is very sad.

04:54.960 --> 04:57.960
Nobody's thinking they're Max Verstappen.

04:57.960 --> 05:02.680
But yeah, pretty boring sprint.

05:02.680 --> 05:07.920
There was a few small incidents, I think Goethe had an incident with Miyata getting a penalty

05:07.920 --> 05:13.480
and Goethe into the pit, I think this reminds me, did you see Goethe's social media after

05:13.480 --> 05:14.480
this?

05:14.480 --> 05:17.920
Yeah, his story, oh my god.

05:17.920 --> 05:22.560
I don't have a story, I wanna put this up on the thing.

05:22.560 --> 05:24.760
It's on Twitter somewhere, I'm sure you'll find it.

05:24.760 --> 05:26.960
I think Hayden posted it.

05:26.960 --> 05:31.840
What do we think of that, cause personally I think, cause that is for your private story,

05:31.840 --> 05:33.840
like close friends, you know.

05:33.840 --> 05:37.560
Yeah, that's embarrassing to post on main in my opinion.

05:37.560 --> 05:44.600
And on main, I mean, yes you are young, but also you are old enough to know better.

05:44.600 --> 05:49.440
And like a level of professionalism that is still needed in the series, I mean.

05:49.440 --> 05:53.960
Like if this was an F4 driver, I'd be like, oh my god, yeah, that's so funny, but you

05:53.960 --> 05:56.120
are in F2.

05:56.120 --> 06:01.240
You've been doing this for several years now, and I mean, this is something, you know, do

06:01.240 --> 06:07.040
it on close friends, they'll have their own private Instagrams, do it on that.

06:07.040 --> 06:13.360
Don’t show it off to everyone, cause it's just immature.

06:13.360 --> 06:16.440
And I don't think Miyata is even doing it on purpose.

06:16.440 --> 06:20.200
Trying to start beef with Miyata is crazy.

06:20.200 --> 06:24.640
It would be like if Victor Martins last year on his social media was just complaining about

06:24.640 --> 06:27.120
Alex Dunne.

06:27.120 --> 06:35.240
I mean, he posted a story before Monaco, Victor, something about memories in Monaco or something

06:35.240 --> 06:36.240
like that.

06:36.240 --> 06:41.640
Memories of turn one last year, something along those lines, I don't remember what it

06:41.640 --> 06:42.640
said exactly.

06:42.640 --> 06:46.640
That's funny though, there's definitely between doing that and doing what Goethe did, which

06:46.640 --> 06:52.760
is like, you know, same guy, that just comes off like being like really like, you know.

06:52.760 --> 06:58.800
It's like when, who was it, when Aron was complaining about Antonelli in that group chat, same sort

06:58.800 --> 06:59.800
of vibes as that.

06:59.960 --> 07:05.520
I think that one was like the same vibe, but I don't think exactly the same style of like,

07:05.520 --> 07:10.280
yeah, you know, like what Goethe did was like, I don't know, he was just a bit annoyed, I

07:10.280 --> 07:11.280
suppose.

07:11.280 --> 07:15.280
So I think moving on to the feature race, as there's literally nothing to say about the

07:15.280 --> 07:20.240
sprint, but the feature race, we should get in a bit of another snooze first.

07:20.240 --> 07:21.240
Until the end.

07:21.240 --> 07:22.240
The end was entertaining.

07:22.240 --> 07:28.680
Yeah, I mean, I mean, the moment they started pitting, I don't know, what happened to Camara?

07:29.320 --> 07:31.480
Either it was a skill issue, or...

07:31.480 --> 07:33.560
Was it a tire temperature thing?

07:33.560 --> 07:37.320
I actually still don't know what happened until now.

07:37.320 --> 07:45.560
Yeah, I mean, we don't officially know, I mean, he was saying he had no grip on the radio.

07:45.560 --> 07:47.560
Engineer's radio was kind of crazy.

07:47.560 --> 07:53.720
Yeah, I don't know how the engineer thought he could get away, you know, saying like his

07:53.720 --> 07:57.400
job is essentially to advise him on like what to do.

07:57.400 --> 08:02.200
And yet, that's the thing, like incredibly, like, how.

08:02.200 --> 08:04.200
It's really unprofessional.

08:04.200 --> 08:05.200
Exactly.

08:05.200 --> 08:10.760
Even in Canada, the engineer was being a bit like, dude, are you okay?

08:10.760 --> 08:11.880
Like, is he okay?

08:11.880 --> 08:12.880
What just happened?

08:12.880 --> 08:20.200
I think Canada was a bit more justified because, you know, Camara's whole wheel-to-wheel situation,

08:20.200 --> 08:22.360
but there was kind of...

08:22.360 --> 08:27.720
Part of your job as an engineer, to any driver in any series, is to be able to calm the driver

08:27.720 --> 08:32.520
down and keep them kind of in check, because as a driver, it is natural for your emotions

08:32.520 --> 08:35.080
to be all over the place, you know, all that adrenaline.

08:35.080 --> 08:39.640
Like you see in F1, GP and Max Verstappen, you know, GP almost has to reign him in.

08:40.760 --> 08:45.240
That is part of the job of the engineer, and there's no need to be being so rude to your

08:45.240 --> 08:48.440
driver and being like, you know, swearing at him.

08:49.160 --> 08:50.280
That's insane.

08:50.280 --> 08:54.440
Don't forget that Camara is paying the team, so therefore, like, the engineer is getting

08:54.440 --> 08:59.480
paid by, like, it's not an F1 situation where the engineers, I guess, they have a bit more,

08:59.480 --> 09:05.880
they have more justification to be more angry at the drivers, like, act like that on the

09:05.880 --> 09:10.600
radio, whereas you in F2, F3, the drivers are paying you, essentially.

09:10.600 --> 09:15.240
It's just a bit of an interesting way of conduct, I suppose.

09:15.640 --> 09:23.320
Tsolov was really impressive for being able to avoid Camara when he was going off, by

09:23.320 --> 09:30.200
the way, like, when the replay played, that it was so close to taking them both out.

09:31.240 --> 09:33.720
So I was really impressed by Tsolov with that.

09:34.280 --> 09:38.920
Yeah, I mean, Tsolov for me, he's been the best driver across every single race,

09:39.560 --> 09:40.680
this whole series.

09:40.680 --> 09:45.800
I mean, I feel like it's just kind of, I mean, it is a weak field, I would say, especially

09:45.800 --> 09:50.520
compared to what we had two years ago, even last year's field was quite stronger than

09:50.520 --> 09:56.840
this, but Tsolov has been extremely impressive, and I think, you know, he's almost tidied up

09:56.840 --> 10:03.480
His act because I think in F3 times he could be a bit, he wasn't consistent enough almost,

10:03.480 --> 10:08.280
but now he's, but in a way that he needs to improve his consistency because he's always

10:08.280 --> 10:13.160
win or DNF, it feels like, I mean the Canada weekend was a nightmare for him.

10:13.880 --> 10:18.040
Yeah, I mean, he's had quite a bit of bad luck in some races,

10:19.000 --> 10:23.320
like when he got taken out by Tasanapol in the Miami feature,

10:24.680 --> 10:27.400
and still after that led the championship.

10:28.440 --> 10:31.880
So yeah, I've been pretty impressed overall by Tsolov.

10:31.880 --> 10:37.240
I mean, I really hope he can be the one to honestly win the title, but given, you know,

10:37.240 --> 10:43.720
our good friend Mecachrome, who knows, because, I mean, Beganovic has also been up there,

10:43.720 --> 10:50.120
and he's also been quite, I think, he's been Mecachromed in both Melbourne and Canada,

10:50.120 --> 10:53.800
and like he's been like kind of slowly there in the background, I mean, he got a podium

10:53.800 --> 10:58.200
again this time, and I think if he were to get some better luck, he could maybe be up there.

10:58.760 --> 11:03.720
Of course, Dunne is another name we can't leave out. He's fast, just messy.

11:03.720 --> 11:08.840
Yeah, but he didn't crash out anyone this weekend, which is great.

11:09.480 --> 11:15.480
If he keeps that up, he could challenge, because I think he is incredibly fast as well.

11:16.280 --> 11:22.040
Do you guys remember in Barcelona last year, where he went into Victor in the pits?

11:22.600 --> 11:24.360
I remember, it was like right after Monaco.

11:25.160 --> 11:30.120
Right after Monaco? I mean, he really had it out for Martins last year, I don't know what it was.

11:31.080 --> 11:35.800
How was there somebody crossing the pit lane, as cars were pitting?

11:35.800 --> 11:42.440
That was so weird. What was that about? And like, the FIA haven't said anything about it at all,

11:42.440 --> 11:43.720
as far as I know.

11:43.720 --> 11:47.720
Even the commentators like glazed over, like when I was watching it, I was like ‘what the hell?’

11:47.720 --> 11:53.960
like I had to rewind to see, did I actually see that, right? I mean, firstly, it's common in

11:53.960 --> 11:59.160
Monaco for cars to wait till the end to pit, I mean, that's a common thing, you need to be

11:59.160 --> 12:06.760
expecting that. And like, surely you'd know, if you're the FIA, oh, these cars still need to pit,

12:06.760 --> 12:11.880
and make sure there's nobody there. And what's that guy doing? Like, does he have a death wish?

12:13.080 --> 12:16.040
It could have ended really bad, and I'm glad that it didn't.

12:17.080 --> 12:21.560
I mean, there needs to be some better safety protocols in place for that not to happen again,

12:21.560 --> 12:22.440
because-

12:22.440 --> 12:23.640
Yeah, exactly.

12:23.640 --> 12:25.400
It wasn't a team personnel, right?

12:25.640 --> 12:29.480
It looked like, I think it was somebody from the FIA, I mean-

12:29.480 --> 12:33.400
That's just even more idiotic, then. Because I thought it could have been, if it was team

12:33.400 --> 12:38.040
personnel, that would make a bit more sense, that it's still idiotic. But if it's like the FIA,

12:38.040 --> 12:42.520
or like, God forbid, it's like, and literally like a member of the public, that's just insane.

12:43.080 --> 12:45.560
Yeah, it's been not too sure, it's not been officially told.

12:46.440 --> 12:52.600
I just know it's some guy in a white shirt, but if a team personnel doesn’t have a team shirt on, then.

12:52.680 --> 12:57.400
Yeah, that's what I thought, but I don't know, it's just a bit crazy to think about.

12:57.400 --> 12:59.160
Well, I don't think you'd see that any other-

12:59.720 --> 13:02.040
Could just be the nature of how Monaco is as a circuit,

13:02.040 --> 13:05.080
and I guess they'll maybe act if they're more easy on them or something, I don't know.

13:05.080 --> 13:07.560
So like, than is a random member of the public got in.

13:07.560 --> 13:11.640
But, or maybe the photographers, well, I mean, I don't think they had a camera on them,

13:11.640 --> 13:14.520
but it could have well been the photographers, I wish.

13:15.080 --> 13:19.160
I remember a few years ago in Baku, in the F1, I think it was Gasly who

13:19.240 --> 13:23.480
Pitted the end, and he had a ton of photographers in the pit lane,

13:23.480 --> 13:26.440
and they were like inches away from his car, and it was like,

13:27.080 --> 13:30.920
I mean, this has happened before, I mean, this needs to get a stricter safety protocol,

13:30.920 --> 13:35.560
because it was fine this time, but next time, I mean, this can't keep on happening.

13:35.560 --> 13:41.960
And looking to the overall standings now, Mini still leads, but Tsolov has closed that gap up

13:41.960 --> 13:48.280
a lot, but we have Stenshorne and Dunne, tied on points, and Leon, fifth, which is a bit of a

13:48.280 --> 13:52.840
surprise to me, I was not really expecting that. He's a bit of a reverse grid merchant.

13:53.560 --> 13:57.160
Agreed. And Camara is now beaten out by him,

13:57.160 --> 14:04.360
I mean, I mean, he lost the podium, maybe Tsolov would have beaten him if he stood on the podium,

14:05.320 --> 14:11.240
who knows, but he lost out on a lot, and it'd be good if we could find out what actually happened

14:11.240 --> 14:14.600
to him. By the way, Tsolov is only one away from Mini,

14:14.680 --> 14:20.280
honestly, I think we're going to see this weekend, maybe Tsolov might overtake Mini

14:20.280 --> 14:24.600
and get that championship lead again. As long as he didn't get any bad luck,

14:24.600 --> 14:28.120
he should be able to. And also to note, Stenshorne and Dunne

14:28.120 --> 14:33.480
are both on equal points. I think Stenshorne’s third, I think because of one of his race wins, but I guess

14:33.480 --> 14:38.920
for both Rodins, it's really just as it goes. They're both pretty good,

14:40.040 --> 14:43.320
and I think they'll both do well in the championship going into next weekend,

14:43.320 --> 14:46.440
or this weekend, I should say. At least the top four, they're all quite

14:46.440 --> 14:48.520
solid, honestly. Yeah, agreed.

14:49.560 --> 14:54.200
Mini, I wouldn't have rated until like, honestly, I don't really rate Mini that well,

14:54.760 --> 15:01.160
so I'm not as inclined to say that he's pretty decent. I mean, this weekend has shown that I

15:01.160 --> 15:03.480
think he is. He's been consistent,

15:03.480 --> 15:10.040
that's what he... Like even in F3, it was like, he was consistent, that's what helped him, so.

15:10.840 --> 15:13.640
And in these championships, ultimately that's what you need to do,

15:13.640 --> 15:17.720
because you can't be like, winning one week, then DNFing the next two.

15:18.760 --> 15:24.440
Let's look over to Formula 3 now, and Formula 3 was quite similar to Formula 2,

15:24.440 --> 15:32.360
and I mean, we had James Wharton with a big crash in practice. He's not been having a good time.

15:33.000 --> 15:38.120
Yeah, that cannot be very good for him after the incident in Australia,

15:38.120 --> 15:46.360
like immediately the next race weekend for him, again, crashes. That cannot be great.

15:47.640 --> 15:49.640
And then with the qualifying, I mean,

15:49.640 --> 15:53.320
I would not expect Brando Badoer to be topping a group.

15:53.960 --> 15:57.080
I don't know anymore.

15:58.520 --> 16:04.200
Like last year, he was, I mean, even though he was in Prema, because he was comfortably

16:04.280 --> 16:09.240
outperformed. But he was outperformed easily by Leon and Ugochukwu.

16:11.240 --> 16:17.160
He's topping his group by three tenths, and you know, later on, wins the feature race.

16:17.160 --> 16:21.480
I'm going to wait a couple more weekends before I judge anything.

16:22.200 --> 16:26.840
And yeah, Fionn McLaughlin continues, I mean, I just feel a bit bad for him,

16:26.840 --> 16:32.040
because we know he's fast, but the season has just been struggle after struggle for him,

16:32.040 --> 16:35.080
it's like, nothing's quite clicked yet for him, almost.

16:35.720 --> 16:38.520
I think we're going to see a lot of this this season.

16:38.520 --> 16:41.720
I think he'll probably come back for another season next year.

16:42.360 --> 16:47.160
And that's when we'll go back to seeing his actual pace.

16:47.160 --> 16:54.120
He may even be very Tsolov-esque, I mean, just Tsolov-esque that could mean doing three seasons in F3.

16:54.120 --> 16:59.400
He's older than Tsolov was when he did F3, though, right?

17:00.360 --> 17:02.440
I think McLaughlin's like 18.

17:02.440 --> 17:07.320
Yeah no, he started F4 a year later than you typically do, so he's about a year older then.

17:08.040 --> 17:08.360
Yeah.

17:09.080 --> 17:13.800
And then yeah, Group B, Nael on top of the group and  took overall pole.

17:13.800 --> 17:18.920
Freddie, of course, I'm trying to talk about Freddie, he was P2 in the group.

17:19.480 --> 17:23.080
He did touch the wall twice on his final run, but managed to keep it, you know,

17:23.720 --> 17:25.080
not damage the car too much.

17:25.960 --> 17:27.480
I don't understand how he did that.

17:28.440 --> 17:31.240
Because he's just, you know, he's really good, that's how.

17:32.200 --> 17:35.000
And I mean, his first time in Monaco, and I really like how he

17:35.000 --> 17:37.480
immediately pushed the limit in his first time here.

17:37.480 --> 17:39.720
Rivera's been pretty impressive.

17:39.720 --> 17:42.520
Yeah, of course, he missed out on Melbourne with injury,

17:42.520 --> 17:48.200
and I mean, to immediately be on the pace, third in the group is really impressive.

17:48.200 --> 17:55.560
I mean, Campos are so good as well, I mean, all their cars in the top five in qualifying.

17:56.200 --> 18:06.120
Yeah, Meanwhile Trident, De Palo is kind of nowhere, and Stromsted is there, I guess.

18:07.080 --> 18:10.280
I mean, Stromsted is always like on the back end of the top ten.

18:11.400 --> 18:15.320
Yeah, at least he's in the top ten, De Palo is, I don't know what he's doing.

18:15.960 --> 18:21.400
I mean, maybe I am biased, because I am a Freddie fan, of course, but I feel like almost this year,

18:21.480 --> 18:25.400
based on the driver performance we've seen from all three of drivers in each team,

18:25.400 --> 18:27.400
I feel like Campos do seem stronger.

18:28.360 --> 18:30.600
I think it will be a tight Campos title.

18:31.240 --> 18:36.840
You know, easily, because also, I mean, I think Campos have, if I just look at drivers overall,

18:36.840 --> 18:42.200
I feel like Campos have a stronger lineup than Trident, because Trident are a bit too reliant

18:42.200 --> 18:48.200
on Freddie, because, yes, De Palo did challenge for the title last year in FRECA, but other than that,

18:48.760 --> 18:50.040
he's not done that much.

18:51.000 --> 18:55.480
And Stromsted is sort of like, he's solid, but that's about it.

18:56.200 --> 18:56.520
Yeah.

18:57.160 --> 19:00.680
Yeah, like, well, at least in F3, the sprint race was quite eventful.

19:01.640 --> 19:10.200
That car park at the hairpin was so, it started off with Taponen going into the side of Rivera,

19:10.200 --> 19:16.360
I think, and then it just, like, everyone behind them had to, like, basically stop.

19:17.080 --> 19:23.240
Benavides hit the wall, and then, no, he hit one of the Dams drivers, I don't remember which one.

19:24.200 --> 19:28.760
Then he ended up in the wall, and then Christian Ho went into the back of him.

19:28.760 --> 19:32.600
He's broken three vertebrae in his back, so I mean, we're wishing him a speedy recovery.

19:32.600 --> 19:36.200
But I do wonder then, who's going to replace him then?

19:36.200 --> 19:41.720
It's concerning that we've had two spine injuries in two rounds.

19:41.720 --> 19:44.120
I mean, we had Sharp in Australia.

19:44.120 --> 19:44.600
Yeah.

19:44.600 --> 19:47.720
And then we have Benavides this weekend.

19:47.720 --> 19:52.600
I feel like this has not happened that much in recent years in F2 and F3.

19:53.240 --> 19:55.480
Yeah, so I don't know what's going on.

19:55.480 --> 19:58.040
And normally when there are injuries, if anything it’s going to be a wrist injury,

19:58.040 --> 20:02.120
it's going to be carrying the steering wheel, a jerk suddenly, that will cause a wrist injury,

20:02.120 --> 20:05.880
but back injury, I mean, it is the impact.

20:05.880 --> 20:09.960
The FIA need to look into this and see why this is happening.

20:10.760 --> 20:15.960
Maybe we need to improve the internal crash structure of the car to kind of shield the driver more.

20:18.120 --> 20:23.080
So Benavides is supposedly supposed to be out for about two months,

20:23.720 --> 20:26.760
which basically puts him out of almost the entire season.

20:28.280 --> 20:35.800
So obviously AIX are going to have a replacement for him, but I have no clue who it could be.

20:35.800 --> 20:40.440
I'm thinking someone who came in last year, you know, someone like Bedrin.

20:41.320 --> 20:48.200
Interestingly, the next two FRECA rounds, the next few F3 rounds, don't collide with FREC.

20:49.400 --> 20:53.720
We could see a FREC driver, but we also could see someone, like, unemployed.

20:53.720 --> 20:57.560
Yeah, because I remember last year, Freddie, raced one race for AIX,

20:57.560 --> 21:04.840
so we could see somebody like, you know, KNB maybe doing one round of- Complete speculation.

21:05.560 --> 21:08.360
Yeah, we don't actually know anything.

21:09.400 --> 21:11.720
The Grand Prixquel podcast is not a news source.

21:12.600 --> 21:16.440
Yeah, we are recording on the Tuesday after Monaco, so...

21:17.160 --> 21:21.320
If they announce it right after we're recording, again, I think I'm going to cry.

21:22.040 --> 21:23.640
British F4, I'm looking at you.

21:25.560 --> 21:32.680
But yeah, so obviously we hope that Brad gets better, cause that's a really serious injury.

21:32.760 --> 21:36.840
And then yeah, Freddie also is a bit of a terrorist that race.

21:36.840 --> 21:41.400
He went into, who was it, like, Rivera and Nael?

21:41.960 --> 21:44.040
What happened is, it was down the hairpin,

21:44.040 --> 21:47.800
Freddie tried to go down the inside of Rivera, but it ended up making contact,

21:47.800 --> 21:50.760
and Nael was just unlucky to be the guy behind Freddie,

21:50.760 --> 21:53.320
not having enough time to react, and went into the back of him.

21:53.320 --> 21:57.960
Poor Rivera. Rivera got terrorized twice in one race.

21:58.600 --> 22:00.360
It's like, why did I come back to this?

22:01.880 --> 22:08.520
And then on the road, Yamakoshi won, but then he had push rods on the wrong side, he got disqualified.

22:09.240 --> 22:11.720
The stewards document was hilarious.

22:12.520 --> 22:19.400
The team were basically trying to argue that the push rods being labeled LH and RH

22:19.960 --> 22:24.040
was unclear, that that meant right hand and left hand.

22:24.600 --> 22:27.960
I feel like even a five year old could figure L.

22:28.840 --> 22:33.320
What does it stand for? Like Lewis Hamilton and Rewis Hamilton?

22:35.800 --> 22:38.600
Yeah, putting Lewis Hamilton onto the car.

22:38.600 --> 22:42.920
I mean that is how you're going to win, it's just Lewis Hamilton in there, he is one of the GOATs.

22:42.920 --> 22:46.680
But yeah, I don't know, the stewards document when I read it,

22:46.680 --> 22:52.520
cause I really do like looking into the stewards documents and seeing what's going on.

22:52.520 --> 22:54.920
Like some of them can be really funny sometimes.

22:55.560 --> 22:57.960
I mean, at least the sprint race was quite eventful.

22:58.920 --> 23:01.560
I mean, not that there was much racing, but we had incidents.

23:02.280 --> 23:04.040
The feature race, however.

23:04.040 --> 23:05.720
Feature race was nothing.

23:06.520 --> 23:11.080
I mean, props to Badoer, he had a great start and that's what won him the race.

23:11.080 --> 23:14.920
I was paying attention here, the top 10 didn't change since the first lap, I swear.

23:15.880 --> 23:17.160
Yeah, sounds about right.

23:18.120 --> 23:20.680
That's the problem with no pit stops in F3.

23:20.680 --> 23:25.640
It could have been really interesting if they did.

23:25.640 --> 23:30.680
And I think maybe after this chaotic sprint race they were maybe being a bit more conservative

23:30.680 --> 23:32.200
and not going for moves.

23:32.200 --> 23:37.320
Yeah, so really the only thing that happened was Taponen was put into the wall by Gladysz.

23:38.280 --> 23:38.780
That's it.

23:39.400 --> 23:42.200
I'm sorry, guys, there's literally no even like,

23:42.200 --> 23:44.440
at least F2 towards the end something happened.

23:44.440 --> 23:47.720
F3 was genuinely putting me to sleep.

23:48.680 --> 23:50.840
The snooze fest, of all snooze fests.

23:51.400 --> 23:53.720
Yeah, but look towards championship.

23:54.360 --> 23:59.960
And still, you know,Ugochukwu, Del Pino, Slater, Badoer, Nile, which almost makes sense.

23:59.960 --> 24:04.920
I mean, Freddy, I mean, he was tied on points with Del Pino before, but then,

24:04.920 --> 24:07.880
I mean, Del Pinois low-key being a sprint merchant.

24:08.520 --> 24:15.080
Yeah, VAR in general have been sprint merchants, but I'll take it if it benefits Enzo.

24:15.720 --> 24:18.520
Well, it's not like he did much this weekend.

24:18.520 --> 24:19.960
Yeah, Enzo was kind of nowhere.

24:21.080 --> 24:26.360
Oh yeah, we forgot to mention the ridiculous amount of penalties after the sprint.

24:26.920 --> 24:30.600
Like half the grid got drive-throughs turned into 30 seconds.

24:31.320 --> 24:37.160
Because what was it like the mechanics touched to the car during the red flag,

24:37.160 --> 24:39.880
or like when they were restarting after the red flag?

24:40.600 --> 24:42.200
Something along those lines.

24:42.200 --> 24:46.680
And like half the grid got drive-throughs turned into 30 seconds for that.

24:46.680 --> 24:53.560
And Enzo also got another 10 seconds on top of that for cutting the corner in turn one

24:54.200 --> 24:55.400
and gaining an advantage.

24:56.040 --> 25:02.360
Yeah, I think this championship is going to involve into Ugochukwu-Slater title fight,

25:02.360 --> 25:09.480
because I mean, Del Pino, I mean, it’s literally sprint race this series, he won the first sprint race in the second this weekend.

25:09.480 --> 25:13.000
And that's getting him a majority of his points.

25:13.000 --> 25:14.680
So that can't last forever.

25:15.480 --> 25:18.760
I'd like to see Nael in the title fight as well, though.

25:18.760 --> 25:24.200
Yeah, I mean, I think he does have the pace, but then maybe, how do Campos play that then?

25:24.920 --> 25:25.880
No idea.

25:25.880 --> 25:28.120
Because also they need to make sure there's no

25:28.120 --> 25:32.200
contact or anything because, you know, Team Championship is on the line as well.

25:32.920 --> 25:37.000
Yeah, I guess we'll see in future rounds what Campos decide to do.

25:37.000 --> 25:40.440
It could be like last year, you know, how Boya ended up like,

25:40.440 --> 25:42.520
what was it, third in the championship by the end?

25:43.160 --> 25:43.880
Yeah.

25:43.880 --> 25:45.320
It could end up like that.

25:46.200 --> 25:48.680
Now moving on to EC3.

25:49.720 --> 25:51.240
Why is Tommy Harfield here?

25:52.200 --> 25:55.880
I need to take a deep breath before we start Eurocup-3.

25:57.080 --> 26:02.920
Because this weekend, this weekend has been a weekend of all time.

26:04.440 --> 26:05.160
Oh my god.

26:05.800 --> 26:08.280
Okay, first of all, Tommy Harfield.

26:08.920 --> 26:11.400
It was nice to see him on the pace.

26:12.600 --> 26:21.480
I was looking through testing during the week because they had testing on Tuesday and Wednesday.

26:22.680 --> 26:27.640
And he was consistently up there in the top 15-ish.

26:28.440 --> 26:32.840
And I was pretty impressed considering, you know, it's his first regional race.

26:33.800 --> 26:38.600
It didn't really translate as well to qualifying and the races,

26:38.600 --> 26:41.320
but I was still pretty impressed by his debut.

26:42.520 --> 26:43.880
So quali one.

26:43.880 --> 26:49.560
We had Feghali on pole and we had three Campos' of Powell,

26:49.560 --> 26:51.640
Strauven and Heuezenroeder in the top 10.

26:52.760 --> 26:59.240
Campos has really locked in finally because, I mean, up until this weekend,

27:00.200 --> 27:04.040
their other drivers other than Powell were kind of nowhere.

27:04.920 --> 27:09.480
So it's nice to see Campos, you know, finally lock in on qualifying.

27:10.040 --> 27:11.720
What is up with Eyckmans, though?

27:12.440 --> 27:14.600
Eyckmans was, I don't know what happened to him.

27:15.640 --> 27:19.480
When I said nowhere on the docs, he was kind of like in the lower top 10.

27:20.200 --> 27:24.360
So he was still there, but not there.

27:24.360 --> 27:26.200
You'd expect him to be top 5.

27:26.840 --> 27:31.240
Yeah, I feel like from our top 4, like we had before the season,

27:31.240 --> 27:32.440
only three of them will perform.

27:32.440 --> 27:35.800
Maybe once someone won, the fourth one won't be there for some reason,

27:35.800 --> 27:37.320
and like going into the season.

27:39.800 --> 27:41.640
It's five drivers.

27:44.440 --> 27:46.600
Have you forgotten Al-Azhari again?

27:46.600 --> 27:47.100
Yes.

27:49.080 --> 27:54.360
Out of the five drivers that, you know, we kind of expected to do well

27:54.360 --> 27:57.400
of Egozi and Al-Azhari, Powell, Eyckmans and Lammers,

27:57.960 --> 28:01.400
it's always one or two of them not there every weekend.

28:02.600 --> 28:04.840
It's like, apart from Egozi, he's always there.

28:05.640 --> 28:10.440
Yeah, Egozi, this is the easiest Egozi championship ever.

28:12.360 --> 28:14.760
If he loses this, imagine the fumble.

28:15.960 --> 28:21.560
Yeah, I mean, I'll get into this in a bit, but the championship lead is insane.

28:22.360 --> 28:24.520
I mean, it’s the Alex Palou Witchcraft.

28:25.080 --> 28:25.800
Exactly.

28:25.800 --> 28:29.560
He's hired the same Etsy witch that Alex Palou has.

28:29.560 --> 28:32.360
I mean, Alex Palou, he races for Palou Motorsport.

28:32.360 --> 28:33.880
Alex just got it for him.

28:34.440 --> 28:35.880
It comes from being part of the team.

28:37.640 --> 28:41.560
Are you actually- I'm actually kind of shocked with this ridiculous Alex Palou luck.

28:41.560 --> 28:44.680
Like he finally had a bit of bad luck the last weekend in Indycar,

28:44.680 --> 28:48.360
but otherwise he's been insanely lucky this season.

28:48.360 --> 28:51.800
Yeah, race one.

28:55.560 --> 28:57.000
It's okay, Rob.

28:57.000 --> 28:58.680
Okay, deep breath.

28:59.800 --> 29:06.520
The race one was horrible for me as a fan, but the racing this weekend was pretty good.

29:07.320 --> 29:10.360
EC3’s always delivered with the races, I mean.

29:10.360 --> 29:14.920
Yeah, the races are great, more interesting than F2 and F3.

29:15.720 --> 29:22.600
However, as a fan, and obviously I really, really like Alex Powell.

29:24.120 --> 29:28.600
So in a four-way fight for the lead, there was a four-way fight for the lead

29:29.160 --> 29:31.640
on the first lap, which was kind of crazy.

29:33.400 --> 29:34.840
F2 and F3 could never.

29:35.640 --> 29:37.640
Four-way fight between, who was it?

29:38.520 --> 29:43.080
I think it was Lammers, Powell, Egozi, and Feghali.

29:43.560 --> 29:43.800
Yeah.

29:44.760 --> 29:46.680
But in that four-way fight for the lead,

29:47.640 --> 29:53.160
Feghali tapped the back of Alex's car, sent him spinning into the barrier,

29:53.160 --> 29:59.800
and Alex managed to continue on, but he came into the pits later and had to retire the car.

29:59.800 --> 30:05.080
I mean, that's, that'd been, I think, he could have won that race, directly in the fight for it.

30:05.720 --> 30:10.040
He could have won, he could have podiumed, he could have gotten a P4.

30:11.000 --> 30:12.760
Anything is better than a DNF.

30:13.240 --> 30:17.000
I reckon he had a solid podium locked in there, possible win.

30:17.000 --> 30:17.800
Exactly.

30:17.800 --> 30:19.720
Based off the pace he's shown over the weekend.

30:20.280 --> 30:24.360
What I'm more upset about is that Feghali got five seconds.

30:25.000 --> 30:27.160
Not even 10 seconds, five.

30:27.880 --> 30:31.000
He fully takes out another driver, it's only five seconds.

30:31.960 --> 30:39.240
The stewards document says that because Alex slowed down a little bit to avoid a collision

30:39.320 --> 30:43.640
with the two guys that were in front, because Alex slowed down,

30:43.640 --> 30:50.440
it caused Feghali to go into the back of him, so there were quote-unquote mitigating factors.

30:51.640 --> 30:54.360
I don't believe that, that is woke nonsense.

30:55.080 --> 30:56.520
Feghali’s paid the stewards off.

30:57.720 --> 31:00.920
This is a joke, by the way, please don't cancel me, guys.

31:01.720 --> 31:07.320
Moving on to later on in the race, because clearly I have not had enough of a bad time.

31:08.200 --> 31:11.160
Strauven and Heuzenroeder were fighting for position,

31:11.160 --> 31:16.760
and Heusenroeder literally just takes Strauven out and gets a 10-second penalty for it.

31:16.760 --> 31:20.760
See how people can get 10-second penalties, Eurocup-3 stewards.

31:21.880 --> 31:22.920
Notice how that's a thing.

31:24.120 --> 31:26.600
And then we had Tommy Harfield into the gravel.

31:27.640 --> 31:31.240
Yeah, and Heuzenroeder also got another post-race penalty

31:31.240 --> 31:33.800
for leaving the track and gaining an advantage,

31:33.800 --> 31:38.040
so he ended up with 20 seconds worth of penalties by the end of the race.

31:38.040 --> 31:43.640
What is it with everyone being penalized in everything this weekend in all the series?

31:43.640 --> 31:44.360
I don't know.

31:45.080 --> 31:48.200
Even like in a Spanish F4 had a ton of penalties.

31:48.200 --> 31:49.880
Is this the weekend of penalties?

31:50.520 --> 31:51.080
I don't know.

31:51.800 --> 31:53.480
No one can behave themselves.

31:54.520 --> 31:59.640
But Al-Azhari stalled the start again, he did this in the first weekend as well,

31:59.640 --> 32:03.320
I don't know why he keeps doing this, but he stalled again.

32:03.880 --> 32:06.200
Someone needs to teach him how to actually start the car.

32:06.760 --> 32:08.920
But he recovered pretty well, I think.

32:09.720 --> 32:12.280
I don't remember where he placed, but-

32:12.280 --> 32:15.560
He did this last time, but they keep on stalling.

32:15.560 --> 32:19.080
There’s only so far you can go is if they're starting at the top five,

32:19.800 --> 32:22.200
it's not like you can make it back to maybe the podium.

32:22.200 --> 32:27.240
They're going to maybe make it into the top ten, but not top five, I reckon.

32:28.280 --> 32:32.840
So yeah, the podium ended up being Egozi, Lammers, and Dall’Agnol.

32:33.800 --> 32:37.960
It was originally Egozi, Lammers, and Feghali, but then Feghali got a penalty,

32:37.960 --> 32:43.240
so then it was Egozi, Lammers, and Heuzenroeder, but then Heuzenroeder also got penalties,

32:43.240 --> 32:46.040
so then it ended up being Egozi, Lammers, and Dall’Agnol.

32:46.920 --> 32:51.800
And yeah, Egozi, after race one, already had a pretty big championship lead

32:51.800 --> 32:57.160
because three out of his four title contenders had really bad races.

32:57.160 --> 33:00.280
Eyckmans had some sort of- I don't know what happened to him,

33:01.160 --> 33:06.120
but I think some sort of engine problem, something, I don't know.

33:06.760 --> 33:14.280
But he also DNF'd alongside Powell, and Al-Azhari stalled the start.

33:14.280 --> 33:21.240
So the only person who did well other than Egozi was Lammers, who got on the podium.

33:22.760 --> 33:28.200
So yeah, even after race one, it's pretty clear to see that Egozi’s kind of the championship

33:28.200 --> 33:28.680
favorite.

33:29.640 --> 33:34.120
I mean, he has like, what, a 50-point lead or something insane?

33:34.840 --> 33:37.400
We'll get into that later, because it's crazy.

33:38.680 --> 33:43.480
Quali two, this time instead of Eyckmans, Lammers was nowhere,

33:43.480 --> 33:45.320
and by nowhere I actually mean nowhere.

33:45.320 --> 33:49.400
He qualified like P19, and I don't know what happened to him.

33:50.920 --> 33:53.080
Egozi was on pole, as usual.

33:53.960 --> 33:58.440
Um, good from Campos again, Powell was P5 this time.

33:59.240 --> 34:00.840
He was P4 for the first race.

34:01.560 --> 34:06.760
He overtook both Palou cars, or two of the three Palou cars ahead of him,

34:06.760 --> 34:10.360
of Perard and Dall’Agnol, and ended up P3.

34:12.680 --> 34:18.920
Then, eventually, as the race went on, and people were fighting for positions,

34:19.880 --> 34:22.360
Perard crashed into the side of Alex.

34:23.320 --> 34:26.200
And put him in the wall, or the barrier.

34:26.760 --> 34:30.200
And I am, I can't do this, I can't do this anymore.

34:32.200 --> 34:34.280
What curse does Alex Powell have on him?

34:34.280 --> 34:36.040
Because even in the winter series, I remember,

34:36.600 --> 34:40.200
like in the Formula Regional Middle East, in EC3 winter,

34:40.920 --> 34:44.840
there's always some subplot going on with him, which is not even his fault.

34:46.040 --> 34:50.280
Sanson went on like a whole monologue about like, Alex's luck,

34:50.280 --> 34:52.600
and like, what does he have to do to get better luck,

34:52.600 --> 34:57.320
and how like, this stuff can't be happening to a nicer guy.

34:57.320 --> 34:59.000
And it made me really upset, and I was like,

35:00.040 --> 35:03.400
I was thinking like, Sanson, please stop, I'm going to start crying.

35:04.680 --> 35:09.480
I actually feel really bad for him, and think the pace has been there for him every round.

35:10.440 --> 35:12.840
I've kind of talked about this a bit before,

35:12.840 --> 35:16.840
but this season is so much better than last year in terms of pace.

35:17.720 --> 35:19.320
We are, we are fast.

35:19.320 --> 35:22.280
We are, we are up there, we can get podiums.

35:22.280 --> 35:27.320
We can get wins, but the luck is non-existent.

35:27.320 --> 35:31.320
Like, I've talked about his luck in F4 Middle East last year,

35:31.960 --> 35:34.120
but this is so much worse.

35:34.680 --> 35:39.240
But yeah, um, Perard got, Perard DNF'd after that,

35:39.240 --> 35:42.120
and got a five place grid drop for the next race,

35:42.680 --> 35:45.640
which is so much better than the five seconds that Feghali got.

35:46.840 --> 35:49.320
I am, I can't do this.

35:50.040 --> 35:52.360
I am on a Palou Motorsport hate watch,

35:53.320 --> 35:56.600
but the thing is, I really like Alex Palou himself, so.

35:57.640 --> 36:01.640
My thought on Alex Palou is, okay, he's a nice guy.

36:01.640 --> 36:04.200
I can't hate watching him because he seems so nice.

36:04.200 --> 36:07.880
I'm like, how is he so happy every time after he wins?

36:07.880 --> 36:08.920
I don't understand.

36:09.640 --> 36:12.760
Yeah, he treats every race win like it's his first,

36:12.760 --> 36:14.360
and I think that's so nice.


36:14.920 --> 36:16.040
It's so sweet.

36:16.040 --> 36:20.280
And I'm like, I can't hate him, but also I want to.

36:21.000 --> 36:22.680
Sorry Alex, if you're listening to this.

36:23.640 --> 36:25.960
Why would Alex Palou listen to our podcast?

36:26.440 --> 36:27.640
We talk about his drivers!

36:29.080 --> 36:32.200
But yeah, so Egozi won the race, Fork found in kitchen.

36:32.840 --> 36:36.840
Lammers really had no pace at all the entire race,

36:36.840 --> 36:39.000
but because of all the collisions and penalties

36:39.000 --> 36:40.600
and all the stuff that happened ahead of him,

36:40.600 --> 36:42.760
I think he ended up P9 by the end.

36:43.320 --> 36:48.040
Poliam ended up being Egozi, Dall’Agnol, and Al-Azhari.

36:49.560 --> 36:54.680
Feghali DNF'd which karma, but there's nothing else I can say.

36:55.320 --> 36:57.240
He just kind of stopped.

36:57.240 --> 36:58.920
I don't actually know what happened to him.

36:59.560 --> 37:01.320
Sanson said it was a puncture,

37:01.320 --> 37:04.520
but after the replay I don't think it was a puncture,

37:04.520 --> 37:05.720
so I don't know what happened to him.

37:06.840 --> 37:11.160
Strauven and Heuzenroeder finished the race with no penalties,

37:11.160 --> 37:14.920
and I think after the penalties for other drivers,

37:14.920 --> 37:16.440
they finished 5th and 6th,

37:17.240 --> 37:21.080
which is really good considering both of them

37:21.080 --> 37:23.960
have kind of been nowhere since the winter series.

37:26.200 --> 37:30.920
I'm sad for Feldmann Neto because he was in the points,

37:30.920 --> 37:32.680
I think he finished P8,

37:32.680 --> 37:36.360
but then got penalized afterwards for overtaking under safety car.

37:37.640 --> 37:38.520
He cannot win.

37:38.680 --> 37:43.800
During Formula Regional Middle East, he was constantly in incidents.

37:43.800 --> 37:47.240
Then as soon as he's about to score points,

37:47.240 --> 37:50.440
whether it's here or whether it's in EuroFormula Open,

37:50.440 --> 37:52.040
something always happens to him.

37:52.760 --> 37:58.520
And then Pradel ended up getting a 2-place drop for Race 2 somehow.

37:58.520 --> 37:59.960
I don't know how that works,

38:00.680 --> 38:06.200
but he dropped 2 places in the final classification for Race 2 for causing a collision.

38:06.760 --> 38:16.200
So overall, I need whatever Etsy witch Egozi has.

38:16.920 --> 38:19.800
A 100% podium race is actually insane.

38:21.080 --> 38:25.800
5 races, 5 races, 3 wins, a P2 and a P3.

38:25.800 --> 38:28.120
I mean, what is this luck?

38:29.000 --> 38:31.800
Before I get into the drivers' standings,

38:32.520 --> 38:35.000
I wanted to mention something that Sanson pointed out,

38:35.720 --> 38:39.320
which was that obviously we went into the season thinking

38:39.320 --> 38:42.280
it would be Campos and MP dominance,

38:42.280 --> 38:44.200
but it's kind of not been.

38:44.200 --> 38:47.720
We've gotten a lot of Palou dominance.

38:47.720 --> 38:49.160
Hitech is up there.

38:49.160 --> 38:52.520
Campos and MP have kind of been nowhere.

38:53.880 --> 38:57.880
Sanson said this, so it's not me being biased by the way,

38:57.880 --> 39:02.040
but Sanson was saying that out of all the Campos and MP drivers,

39:02.040 --> 39:06.120
the only driver who we've been consistently seeing pace from is Powell.

39:07.240 --> 39:10.200
The others have been nowhere, or not nowhere.

39:10.200 --> 39:12.120
Eyckmans and Lammers have been up there,

39:12.120 --> 39:16.760
but in general, Powell is the best out of all of them,

39:16.760 --> 39:21.720
and because of his own bad luck, we haven't really seen a lot.

39:23.000 --> 39:25.320
Again, not my words, Sanson's.

39:26.120 --> 39:27.640
I really like Jake Sanson.

39:28.280 --> 39:31.720
He's one of the top Alex Powell glazers out there,

39:31.720 --> 39:33.480
and I really appreciate that.

39:34.040 --> 39:35.480
I wonder why you like him, then.

39:37.720 --> 39:40.680
Totally not related to him liking Alex Powell, is it?

39:40.680 --> 39:42.040
Definitely not.

39:42.040 --> 39:44.200
But yeah, so Championship standings.

39:44.200 --> 39:49.080
Egozi leads, obviously, on 106 points.

39:49.080 --> 39:49.580
P2?

39:50.200 --> 39:51.880
How many points does P2 have?

39:51.880 --> 39:53.080
62.

39:53.080 --> 39:54.280
He's actually insane.

39:55.240 --> 39:55.880
What?

39:55.880 --> 39:59.320
It's just, I mean, Al-Azhari hasn't helped himself with stalling twice.

39:59.880 --> 40:00.380
Yeah.

40:01.720 --> 40:04.840
So Al-Azhari is in P2 on 62 points.

40:05.400 --> 40:07.480
Interestingly, something I noticed is,

40:08.120 --> 40:12.360
Egozi and Al-Azhari are the only two to have scored in every race.

40:12.360 --> 40:17.400
Oh, yeah, that makes sense considering their winter series performance.

40:18.200 --> 40:20.840
But Egozi has 100% podium rate.

40:21.960 --> 40:25.720
So P3 is Lammers on 50 points,

40:25.720 --> 40:28.200
P4, Eyckmans on 46,

40:28.280 --> 40:30.520
and P5, Dall’Agnol on 42.

40:31.080 --> 40:32.120
That's the top five.

40:32.920 --> 40:37.240
Other drivers that I wanted to mention, aka the Campos drivers,

40:37.240 --> 40:39.640
Powell is P6 on 27 points.

40:40.200 --> 40:44.600
A lot of the points that he lost were obviously not his fault with the two DNFs this weekend.

40:45.160 --> 40:47.560
He really should have more than 27 points.

40:48.120 --> 40:50.760
But whatever, what can we do about it?

40:50.760 --> 40:53.800
He probably lost at least 30 points this weekend, I feel like.

40:54.360 --> 40:54.860
Yeah.

40:55.580 --> 41:00.060
Um, so Strauven is P11 on 13 points,

41:00.060 --> 41:02.860
Tarnvanichkul is P12 on 12 points,

41:02.860 --> 41:05.660
and Heuzenroeder is P15 on 9 points.

41:06.220 --> 41:09.500
So not a great time for the Campos drivers.

41:10.780 --> 41:13.980
I don't actually remember what the Teams Championship looks like.

41:13.980 --> 41:15.340
Give me two seconds.

41:16.380 --> 41:17.500
Teams Championship.

41:17.500 --> 41:20.700
Palou Motorsport leads on 150 points,

41:21.340 --> 41:23.980
MP Motorsport is on 106,

41:24.620 --> 41:26.300
and Hitech is on 77.

41:27.340 --> 41:31.100
Campos is on 58 in P4.

41:31.900 --> 41:33.500
It has not been a great time.

41:33.500 --> 41:37.660
We're the last out of the actually competent teams.

41:37.660 --> 41:41.980
They were saying that MP Motorsport has the same number of points as Egozi.

41:42.620 --> 41:43.340
My god.

41:43.980 --> 41:47.260
He would be leading the Teams Championship by himself.

41:47.260 --> 41:48.540
It's insane.

41:48.540 --> 41:52.780
So overall, what we've learned is that Eurocup-3 is not a real championship,

41:52.780 --> 41:54.460
and it cannot hurt me.

41:54.460 --> 41:55.980
What's not real cannot hurt me.

41:57.500 --> 42:01.340
We also had a few other F4 series racing this weekend,

42:01.340 --> 42:02.940
which we can give in a brief mention.

42:02.940 --> 42:08.940
So in Spanish F4, we still have Rocco Coronel leading the Championship.

42:09.500 --> 42:11.980
However, his margin has been cut down a lot.

42:12.620 --> 42:14.300
But it's quite an interesting weekend,

42:14.300 --> 42:15.900
because I feel like whoever won was not.

42:15.900 --> 42:20.620
I mean, they had Kasper Schormans and Borys Lyzen winning races.

42:21.260 --> 42:23.740
But I mean, you'd kind of like in the winter series.

42:24.380 --> 42:26.140
It is very much Vivek Kanthan,

42:26.140 --> 42:29.580
and Nathan Tye seemed like would be main challengers.

42:29.580 --> 42:31.580
And Noah Monteiro as well.

42:31.580 --> 42:32.300
And Monteiro.

42:32.300 --> 42:36.220
But they, along with Coronel, weren't really anywhere this weekend.

42:36.220 --> 42:37.900
I mean, Coronel still got a podium.

42:38.460 --> 42:41.020
He inherited it after penalties.

42:41.020 --> 42:43.660
I think he finished that race originally in P5.

42:44.380 --> 42:46.380
The races were really interesting,

42:46.380 --> 42:50.140
and I'd recommend if you got bored of F2 and F3,

42:50.140 --> 42:53.980
I do recommend going back and rewatching the Spanish F4 races,

42:54.540 --> 42:57.580
because there was a lot going on.

42:57.580 --> 43:00.140
We had multiple last lap shootouts,

43:01.020 --> 43:05.900
and a last lap shootout in an F4 series is never a good idea.

43:07.340 --> 43:09.020
Other than Spanish F4.

43:09.020 --> 43:12.220
We also had CEZ F4 on this weekend.

43:13.500 --> 43:16.300
The main thing I wanted to talk about was Matilda Paatz.

43:16.940 --> 43:24.380
She was in P2 in race 1, but got spun out by another driver.

43:25.020 --> 43:29.980
Ended up in last place, continued the race, recovered up to P14.

43:31.020 --> 43:33.340
Which, I was really impressed by that.

43:33.340 --> 43:36.540
Obviously, I got really sad that she missed out on a podium.

43:36.540 --> 43:41.020
And these CEZ F4 races, there's no reverse grids.

43:41.660 --> 43:43.340
It's on merit.

43:43.340 --> 43:45.420
She qualified, I think she qualified P4.

43:47.260 --> 43:49.500
Went up to P2, got crashed out,

43:50.860 --> 43:54.700
went back to last place, and then recovered to P14.

43:56.460 --> 43:59.660
And then finished race 2 in P4.

43:59.660 --> 44:02.780
She was fighting for a podium at some point.

44:03.580 --> 44:05.420
Finished the race P5 on the road,

44:05.420 --> 44:08.380
but then someone ahead of her got penalized, so she ended up P4.

44:09.500 --> 44:14.860
And I've been really impressed by her here and in F1 Academy.

44:15.020 --> 44:18.300
So, yeah, and I think looking towards the championship,

44:18.940 --> 44:21.580
Walther has taken the lead back from Elia Weiss,

44:21.580 --> 44:24.700
but I mean, they're quite- they’re only about 10 points apart

44:24.700 --> 44:27.500
from each other in the standings, so it's not too far apart.

44:28.380 --> 44:34.220
The thing is about the CEZ standings, is that the results of race 4 are provisional,

44:34.220 --> 44:39.820
because there are 11 drivers who are currently under investigation

44:39.820 --> 44:42.780
for some sort of technical infringement,

44:42.780 --> 44:47.740
where the steward's document says that whoever is investigating them,

44:47.740 --> 44:51.740
is unsure whether the cars conform to the regulations or not.

44:52.460 --> 44:55.340
What do you mean you're unsure? Either they conform or they don't.

44:56.220 --> 44:57.740
They're just making it up as they go.

44:58.780 --> 45:06.380
Yeah, so there's 11 drivers who we don't know if their cars conform or not.

45:06.940 --> 45:13.100
So we'll obviously have to wait and see what comes from that investigation,

45:13.100 --> 45:14.780
because again, it is only Tuesday.

45:15.420 --> 45:20.700
The race weekend ended two days ago, so we still don't know anything yet.

45:20.700 --> 45:22.780
The results are still provisional as of now.

45:24.780 --> 45:26.300
We'll have to wait and see, I guess.

45:27.660 --> 45:30.220
Central European Zone F4 is not a real series.

45:31.020 --> 45:31.820
What is happening?

45:32.940 --> 45:35.180
And I think that's it for today's episode.

45:35.660 --> 45:37.340
Thank you guys for listening.

45:37.340 --> 45:40.780
If you have any comments or anything else you'd like to let us know,

45:40.780 --> 45:44.940
make sure to follow us and let us know on all our socials,

45:45.500 --> 45:47.980
Twitter, Instagram, and TikTok at grandprixquel.

45:48.780 --> 45:55.340
Our next episode will be a recap of the upcoming F2 and F3 weekends at Barcelona,

45:55.340 --> 45:56.860
so make sure to tune in for those.

45:56.860 --> 45:57.980
Thank you for listening.


